  "query_preview": "..\n(Today through next Saturday)\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Taking a quick look out the window, as \nfar as the eye can see there is\u2026 nothing. That\u2019s right, the \nprecipitation has cleared up, and it looks to stay that way for \nthe remainder of the weekend. This is thanks to the one weather \npattern no meteorologist in their right mind has ever claimed to \nget into this field for: a ridge. So, as of now, all we can do is \ntake the advice of the Dire Straits and do the Walk of Life \nthrough this weekend, as we wait eagerly for our next wave of \nsnow. So if you are looking for what to expect in between, you \nhave come to the right place. Thanks to this convenient ridge, our\nhighs will be continuing on an upward trend, with Monday being \nour only exception. Highs for today are currently expected to sit\nin the low 40s for the valleys on Saturday, and the 30s for the \nhigher elevations. This does in fact mean that those of us who \nstill have our winter wonderlands will be watching them disappear \nas Saturday progresses. And as nice as it would be to say that \nlatent cooling effects from the melting snow will have an impact \non temperatures, keeping us significantly lower, due to the ridge \ngiving us mostly clear skies, these impacts will be severely \nlimited. This will be the story for most of the weekend. \n\nA brief shortwave is expected to pass through Saturday night, but\ndue to limited moisture and instability values, precipitation is \nnot expected to take place at this time. Instead, we will be \ngifted with some light cloud cover, keeping the overnight low a \nbit warmer than what could have been. \n\nThis pattern is expected to last us until Sunday night, when we \ncan fill our souls with some short lived giddy once again. \nRemember back when I said Monday would be the exception to our \nwarming trend? A fast moving trough is currently forecast to work \nits way into the CWA Sunday night, and with it, a very weak cold \nfront, though a full on front may be strong wording in this case. \nIt will mostly be just a short burst of cold air that brings out \nthe best in our instability and small amounts of moisture, meaning\nwe will have the potential for some rain and snow showers Sunday \nnight and Monday morning. Scattered showers are expected mainly\nnorth of I-80 within our CWA. Current model guidance suggests \nprecipitation totals will be relatively low, with expected snow \namounts between 0-0.5\u201d. Monday we will see the highs in the \nvalleys in the mid to upper 30s, with lows in the teens and 20s, \nbut afterwards we see yet another ridge set in very quickly and \nthe warming trend continues. By the end of the week, we may even \nsee highs in the mid 50s, with lows above freezing. For the highs,\nthat is only slightly above normal, but for the lows, that is \nsubstantially higher. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High confidence exists in the \ngeneral warming trend. Low confidence exists in precipitation\ntype, timing, and coverage for Sunday into Monday.",
